RMDIR(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     RMDIR(1)

NAME
    rmdir — Remove a directory and everything inside it, because
            apparently "rmdir" has chosen violence.

SYNOPSIS
    rmdir <dir>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'rmdir' command removes the specified directory recursively.

    And by recursively, we mean:

        "The directory, its children, their children, and anything else
         hiding in there are all going away."

    Unlike 'rm', which politely refuses to touch directories, 'rmdir'
    has no such hesitation.

    There is no confirmation prompt.

    There is no recycle bin.

    There is no undo.

    There is only 'shutil.rmtree()' and your rapidly increasing heart rate.

ARGUMENTS
    dir
        The directory to remove.

        Required. If omitted, the command displays its usage and
        deletes absolutely nothing. A rare moment of restraint.

ERRORS
    No such directory: <path>
        The requested directory does not exist.

        Good news: your destructive ambitions have been thwarted.

    rmdir error: <error>
        Something went wrong while removing the directory.

        Possible causes include:
        - Permission denied.
        - The filesystem object is having a bad day.
        - Something inside the directory refused to cooperate.
        - You have angered the filesystem gods.

OUTPUT
    On successful removal:

        Removed <path>

    This means the directory and everything beneath it have been
    removed.

    If you needed anything from there, this is an excellent time
    to remember the importance of backups.

EXAMPLES
    > rmdir old-project
        Removed old-project

        Years of questionable code: gone.

    > rmdir build
        Removes the entire build directory.

        Clean build. Clean conscience.

    > rmdir node_modules
        Removed node_modules

        Your disk space has been liberated.

    > rmdir important-folder
        Removed important-folder

        Was it important?

        It is now a historical question.

    > rmdir missing-folder
        No such directory: ...

        The command searched for your target and found only sadness.

FUN FACTS
    - Despite its name, this command does not merely remove empty
      directories.
    - It uses recursive removal, so non-empty directories are fair game.
    - There is no confirmation flag.
    - There is no "please think about this" flag.
    - Unlike 'rm', directories are its entire personality.
    - If you accidentally delete the wrong directory, 'rmdir' will
      not apologize.

